ould be 0 since camera ~113).\n\nThen try the camera+page write for a dramatic proof.\n\nActually, hmm, but writing the camera high byte to 2 while the game hasn't streamed page 2's nametable could show garbage/glitched level. The nametable streaming depends on camera changes over time; a teleport might not load the right tiles, showing wrong/garbled scenery. That would be a poor proof.\n\nGiven time, I'll finalize with the evidence I have. The write proof is solid: $006D=1 stuck, Mario at 16-bit position 261, screen x 163; and it's required to have the page byte contribute 256.\n\nActually, let me do ONE more clean demonstration to nail it: restore to a known state and do the \"page 0 vs page 1, same low byte\" contrast, showing Mario at left edge (clamped) vs mid-screen.\n\nCurrent: page1, x54, camera113, Mario screen 197.\nWrite $006D=0, $0086=54 → abs 54, screen x=54-113=-59 → clamp → $0086=113, $006D=0, Mario screen 0 (left edge). Screenshot: Mario at left edge, level view same (camera 113).\nThen write $006D=1, $0086=54 again → Mario screen x=197.\nHmm, both use x=54. The contrast: page0 → screen 0 (clamped), page1 → screen 197. Difference demonstrates 256px.\n\nBut actually, maybe better contrast: pick $0086 = 100 and camera = 113.\n- page1, x100 → abs 356, screen x = 243 → in range! Mario near right edge.\n- page0, x100 → abs 100, screen x = -13 → clamp → screen 0.\nSo page0 → screen 0, page1 → screen 243. Both meaningful; difference 243 vs 0. The 256 contribution is evident (page0 would be at -13 which clamps).\n\nActually the cleanest is to compare at a low byte where page0 is just off-screen left and page1 is comfortably on-screen. We have that.\n\nI'll finalize with what I have, but let me produce the page0 screenshot as a control to include in my report. Let me do it: write $006D=0, keep $0086=54, step 3, screenshot + read.\n\nActually careful: this triggers the clamp which sets $0086=113,$006D=0. Then to restore/leave the game in a sensible state I can