{"type":"text","text":"## Found: `$006D` — Mario's horizontal-position **page byte**\n\n**Address:** `$006D` (CPU RAM, 1 byte). **Encoding:** Mario's absolute level x = `$006D * 256 + $0086` — it's the high byte of a 16-bit horizontal position; `$0086` is the low byte that wraps every 256 px. Each unit of `$006D` is one screen.\n\n### How I found it (differential search, no reference)\nI pressed Start twice into World 1-1 (confirmed by screenshot), then staged a clean 256-px crossing rather than free-running:\n\n- **Watch while walking right:** as `$0086` ran `249,250,252,253,255,0,2,3`, `$006D` ran `0,0,0,0,0,1,1,1` — it ticks on exactly the wrap frame. Walking back left, `$0086` ran `…1,0,255,254…` and `$006D` went `1→0` on the same wrap.\n- **Round 1** (`snapshot a` at x=247 → `play_input` Right 8 → `snapshot b`, filter `increased`, name `d1`): **54 survivors**, including `$006D` old 0 → new 1.\n- **Round 2** (`snapshot d` → walk Left 60 → `snapshot e`, filter `decreased`, `from: d1`, name `d2`): **24 survivors**. Raw result trimmed:\n  ```\n  address  old  new    address  old  new\n  0x0005   112   67     0x0342   207  200\n  0x006D     1    0     0x0344   160    0\n  0x02DD   113  112     0x071C  ...  ... (scroll didn't decrease)\n  ```\n  `$006D` is the only survivor that moves by exactly ±1 and only at the 256-px boundary; the rest are OAM/enemy/scroll positions.\n\n### Disassembly confirmation\nThe player-position update is `STA $0086,X ; LDA $006D,X ; ADC $02 ; STA $006D,X` at CPU `$BF40`–`$BF46` — a carry added into a page byte. A separate routine (`$B013`–`$B021`) copies the camera's 16-bit position (`$071A` high, `$071C` low) into `$0086`/`$006D` as an anti-off-screen clamp.\n\n### Write proof\nMid-level with the camera at `$071C`=93, I paused and wrote `$006D`=1 with `$0086`=5. After stepping frames, RAM still held **`$006D`=1, `$0086`=5** (not zeroed) → absolute x 261, and the screenshot showed Mario at on-screen x ≈ 163 (right-hand side, by the hill/Goomba) — not at the left edge, where a page-0 value (abs 5 − 93 = −93) would be clamped. Writing `$006D` alone to a value that leaves Mario off-screen is actively undone by the clamp (it rewrites `$0086`=scroll, `$006D`=0), so the low byte has to be written to match.\n\n### Recorded\n`record_mapping` (type address, name `player_x_page`, source \"differential search\", confidence 1) plus a note correcting the older `$0086` entry's claim that \"no separate player page byte was found\" — `$006D` is that byte. `promote_mappings` ran: file now holds 11 addresses. Read-only file policy was respected (mapping writes went through the tool, which succeeded)."}
